---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { LIMITS } from "../lib/api";
import { DRAND_RELAYS, QUICKNET } from "../lib/drand";
import { formatUtc } from "../lib/time";

const genesis = formatUtc(QUICKNET.genesis_time * 1000);
const maxYears = LIMITS.maxUnlockSeconds / (365 * 86400);
const toc = [
	["steps", "How it works in four steps"],
	["network", "The network: drand quicknet"],
	["who-sees-what", "Who sees what"],
	["privacy", "What we store"],
	["offline", "Encrypt offline"],
	["limitations", "Limitations"],
	["reading", "Further reading"],
];
---

<Base
	title="How it works · timelock.dev"
	description="How timelock.dev locks messages until a future time with drand quicknet and tlock: the method, the network, what we see and store, and the limitations."
>
	<div class="container layout">
		<article class="layout-main markdown">
			<header class="page-head">
				<h1>How it works</h1>
				<p class="lead">
					timelock.dev uses tlock, an encryption method built on the <a href="https://drand.love/">drand</a> network. You encrypt a message to a
					future time. drand creates and publishes the key for that time when it arrives. Until then the key does not exist, so nobody can read
					the message, including you and us.
				</p>
			</header>

			<h2 id="steps">How it works in four steps</h2>
			<ol>
				<li>
					drand is a network run by the <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/leagueofentropy/">League of Entropy</a>, a group of independent
					organizations. Every {QUICKNET.period} seconds the members work together to sign the next round number. No single member can make the
					signature alone.
				</li>
				<li>
					That signature is the key for anything encrypted to its round. To encrypt to a future round, you only need drand's public key, which
					is built into this site. In technical terms, this is identity-based encryption (Boneh–Franklin on the BLS12-381 curve) with the round
					number as the identity.
				</li>
				<li>
					Your message is encrypted with a random key using <a href="https://age-encryption.org/v1">age</a> (ChaCha20-Poly1305). That random key
					is then encrypted to the round you picked. The result is a normal age file with a <code>tlock</code> line:
					<code>-&gt; tlock &lt;round&gt; &lt;chain hash&gt;</code>.
				</li>
				<li>When the round arrives, drand publishes its signature. Anyone with the encrypted file can download it, recover the random key and read the message.</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				The method comes from the paper <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/189">tlock: Practical Timelock Encryption from Threshold BLS</a>
				(Gailly, Melissaris and Romailler, 2023). This site uses the drand team's <a href="https://github.com/drand/tlock-js">tlock-js</a>
				library. Its files work with <a href="https://github.com/drand/tlock">tlock</a> (Go) and
				<a href="https://github.com/thibmeu/tlock-rs">tlock-rs</a> or <a href="https://github.com/thibmeu/drand-rs">dee</a> (Rust), in both
				directions.
			</p>

			<h2 id="network">The network: drand quicknet</h2>
			<div class="table-wrap">
				<table>
					<tbody>
						<tr><th>Chain hash</th><td><code>{QUICKNET.hash}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><th>Public key</th><td><code>{QUICKNET.public_key}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><th>Scheme</th><td><code>{QUICKNET.schemeID}</code> (unchained, signatures on G1, RFC 9380 hashing)</td></tr>
						<tr><th>Period</th><td>{QUICKNET.period} seconds</td></tr>
						<tr><th>Genesis</th><td>{genesis} (round 1)</td></tr>
						<tr><th>Relays</th><td>{DRAND_RELAYS.map((r, i) => <><code>{r}</code>{i < DRAND_RELAYS.length - 1 ? ", " : ""}</>)}</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>
				Round <em>r</em> is published at <code>genesis + (r − 1) × {QUICKNET.period} s</code>. These settings are built into the site and the API.
				Every signature downloaded from a relay is checked against the public key. A relay can delay a round or refuse to send it, but it cannot
				fake one.
			</p>

			<h2 id="who-sees-what">Who sees what</h2>
			<div class="table-wrap">
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th></th><th>Website</th><th>JSON API and original endpoints</th><th>Offline tool</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><th>Where encryption happens</th><td>In your browser</td><td>On our Cloudflare Worker</td><td>On your computer</td></tr>
						<tr><th>We see your message</th><td>No</td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, in memory during the request</td><td>No</td></tr>
						<tr><th>We see the encrypted text</th><td>No</td><td>Yes, in memory during the request</td><td>No</td></tr>
						<tr><th>drand relays see</th><td>Your IP address and the round you decrypt</td><td>Our Worker asking for the round</td><td>Your IP address and the round</td></tr>
						<tr><th>Shown in recent activity</th><td>Type, round and time, reported by your browser</td><td>Type, round and time</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>Encrypting needs no network connection, because the quicknet public key is built into the page. Decrypting downloads one public signature from a drand relay.</p>

			<h3 id="privacy">What we store</h3>
			<p>
				We store one row per encryption or decryption in a Cloudflare D1 database: encrypt or decrypt, website or API, the server time, the drand
				round and its unlock time. The <a href="/#activity">recent activity</a> list on the homepage shows these rows. We do not store or log your
				message, the encrypted text, IP addresses, browser details or cookies. The site has no analytics and no third-party scripts. Like every
				site on Cloudflare, requests pass through Cloudflare's network, which processes connection data for security.
			</p>

			<h2 id="offline"><span id="cli"></span>Encrypt offline</h2>
			<p>
				For anything important, encrypt on your own computer. Then your message never touches a website. With
				<a href="https://github.com/thibmeu/drand-rs/releases">dee</a> (prebuilt for Linux, macOS and Windows):
			</p>
			<pre><code># once: point dee at quicknet
dee remote add quicknet https://api.drand.sh/{QUICKNET.hash}

# encrypt for 30 days (also accepts a round number or a date like "2030-01-01 00:00:00")
dee crypt -a -r 30d message.txt > message.age

# decrypt after the unlock time
dee crypt --decrypt message.age</code></pre>
			<p>Files made with dee open on this site, and files made here open with dee.</p>

			<h2 id="limitations">Limitations</h2>
			<p>Read these before you lock up anything that matters.</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>It depends on drand.</strong> Your message opens only when drand publishes your round. If quicknet pauses, the message stays
					locked until it restarts. If drand shuts down for good before your unlock time, nobody can ever open the message. Quicknet has run since
					{genesis.slice(0, 10)}, but nobody can promise it will run for decades, so be careful with long locks. This site allows up to
					{maxYears} years.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Nobody can open it early.</strong> Not you, not us, and not if you change your mind. There is no reset and no recovery. If you
					lose the encrypted text, the message is gone.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>You trust most drand members.</strong> The lock holds as long as fewer than the threshold of League of Entropy members work
					together to cheat. If enough of them did, they could create future keys and open messages early. See drand's
					<a href="https://docs.drand.love/docs/timelock-encryption/#️-security-assumptions">security assumptions</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The API sees your message.</strong> The JSON API encrypts on our server, so your message passes through it. We don't store or
					log it, but you have to take our word for that. The website and offline tools don't have this problem.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The website trusts the code we send.</strong> The website encrypts in your browser, so we never receive your message. But if
					someone took over this site, they could send code that leaks it. For high-stakes secrets, use an offline tool.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Times round up to {QUICKNET.period} seconds.</strong> Unlock times move up to the next round. Unlocking follows drand's clock,
					not your device's. A wrong device clock only changes the countdown you see.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Some details are public.</strong> Anyone can encrypt anything to any round, so an encrypted message doesn't prove who wrote it
					or when. Anyone with the encrypted text can see its unlock round and roughly how long the message is.
				</li>
				<li><strong>Not quantum-safe.</strong> The method relies on pairings over the BLS12-381 curve, which a large quantum computer could break.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Activity numbers are approximate.</strong> Browsers report website events themselves, and we can't check them. Treat the counts
					as a rough guide.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Size limits.</strong> The JSON API takes messages up to {LIMITS.plaintextBytes / 1024} KiB, and the original endpoints take up to
					{LIMITS.legacyBodyBytes / 1024 / 1024} MiB. Use an offline tool for bigger files.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 id="reading">Further reading</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><a href="https://docs.drand.love/docs/timelock-encryption/">drand: timelock encryption</a> and its <a href="https://docs.drand.love/docs/timelock-encryption/#-use-cases">use cases</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/189">tlock: Practical Timelock Encryption from Threshold BLS</a> (paper)</li>
				<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/League_of_Entropy">League of Entropy</a> on Wikipedia</li>
				<li><a href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/harnessing-office-chaos">Harnessing office chaos</a>: Cloudflare on randomness and timelock encryption</li>
				<li><a href="https://gwern.net/self-decrypting">Gwern: Time-lock encryption</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://people.csail.mit.edu/rivest/pubs/RSW96.pdf">Rivest, Shamir and Wagner: Time-lock puzzles and timed-release crypto</a> (1996)</li>
				<li><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20231207001505/http://www.eecs.harvard.edu/~cat/tlc.pdf">Rabin and Thorpe: Time-lapse cryptography</a> (archived)</li>
				<li><a href="https://sarcophagus.io/">Sarcophagus</a>: a dead man's switch built on smart contracts</li>
			</ul>
		</article>

		<aside class="layout-side sticky toc-side" aria-label="On this page">
			<nav class="Box">
				<div class="Box-header compact"><h2 class="Box-title">On this page</h2></div>
				<ul class="toc">
					{toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
				</ul>
			</nav>
		</aside>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	@media (max-width: 1011px) {
		.toc-side {
			display: none;
		}
	}
</style>
